      ":question/id": "a959a07a-ae37-59fb-85ec-1a2785aa394a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y\u2032=- \\frac{4 x}{3 y^{2}}.$ Work on intervals where $y\\ne 0$; cube roots are real.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\u2032 & =- \\frac{4 x}{3 y^{2}} \\\\ 3 y^{2}\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =- 4 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3 y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int - 4 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ y^{3} & =- 2 x^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution in implicit form is\n\n$$\ny^{3}=- 2 x^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ Solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{C - 2 x^{2}}.\n$$\n\nHere $C$ is an arbitrary real constant.",
